   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let h be a function with a continuous derivative such that \\( h(2) = 3 \\), \\( h(4) = 7 \\), and \\( \\int_{2}^{4} h(x) \\, dx = 9 \\). Find the value of \\( \\int_{2}^{4} x \\, h'(x) \\, dx \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 13
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = x \\) and \\( dv = h'(x) \\, dx \\), so \\( du = dx \\) and \\( v = h(x) \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then the integral equals \\( \\left[x \\, h(x)\\right]_{2}^{4} - \\int_{2}^{4} h(x) \\, dx \\), and the boundary term is \\( 4(7) - 2(3) = 22 \\).",
         "mathjson": 22
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The remaining integral is given: \\( \\int_{2}^{4} h(x) \\, dx = 9 \\).",
         "mathjson": 9
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So the value is \\( 22 - \\left(9\\right) = 13 \\).",
         "mathjson": 13
      }
   ],
